Espresso is a whole different game from the other brew methods. It takes more effort, more time to learn and more money than anything else in this series. It's also where you get the shots and milk drinks you'd get at a café, and plenty of people find dialing it in is half the fun.

This is part seven of [Brew Methods](/coffee/guides/coffee-brew-methods/).

## What makes it different

Every other method lets water flow through the coffee on its own, or with a gentle push. Espresso uses a machine to force hot water through a tightly packed puck of very finely ground coffee at about 9 bars of pressure, roughly nine times the air pressure around you. The whole shot takes about half a minute.

That pressure pulls out a lot of flavor very fast, and it brings oils along with it. You get a small, thick, intense shot with a layer of crema on top.

It also makes everything more sensitive. With pour-over, a slightly-off grind still makes a decent cup. With espresso, a small change in grind can take a shot from great to sour or bitter.

## What you need

Here's where the effort and expense come in. A real espresso setup has a few parts:

- **An espresso machine.** Prices go from a few hundred dollars to several thousand. Entry-level machines can make very good shots, but they take more care and skill.
- **An espresso grinder.** Just as important as the machine, and often worth spending about as much on. Most grinders made for drip and pour-over can't grind fine enough, or can't make small enough changes. Some hand grinders can, and I listed a couple in my [hand grinder review](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/).
- **A scale** that fits under the portafilter, and reads to a tenth of a gram if you can.
- **A tamper** that fits your basket. Most machines come with one, though not always a good one.
- **A milk pitcher**, if you want lattes and cappuccinos.

Then there's the time. Expect to go through some beans learning to dial in, and expect each new bag to need a few shots to get right. There's also a little cleanup and regular maintenance.

## A first shot

Espresso is measured a little differently from other methods. Instead of the water you put in, you weigh the coffee that comes out. A **1:2 ratio** means 18 g of ground coffee makes about 36 g of espresso in the cup.

<CoffeeBrewRecipe {...recipe} />

Fresh beans matter more for espresso than for anything else, but very fresh beans can be hard to work with too. Many people find espresso tastes best from about a week to a few weeks after the roast date. Medium and darker roasts are also easier to start with than light roasts.

## If you're not ready for all that

If you want espresso drinks but not the project, a good [capsule machine](/coffee/guides/capsule-coffee-machines/) with specialty pods gets you a long way. It won't beat a well-dialed espresso machine, but it's fast, consistent and a lot cheaper to get started. I review one in [Milton Line1](/coffee/reviews/milton-line1-capsule-espresso/).

The [AeroPress](/coffee/guides/aeropress-coffee/) can also make a strong, concentrated coffee that works well with milk. It's not true espresso, since it doesn't have the pressure, but it's close enough for a lot of people.

That's the end of Brew Methods. To compare them all again, head back to the [overview](/coffee/guides/coffee-brew-methods/).
